<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<phpunit xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="vendor/phpunit/phpunit/phpunit.xsd"
         bootstrap="tests/bootstrap.php"
         colors="true"
         failOnWarning="true"
         failOnRisky="true"
         enforceTimeLimit="true"
         defaultTimeLimit="60"
         cacheDirectory=".phpunit.cache">
    <!--
        defaultTimeLimit is a hang-catcher, not a performance budget. It exists
        because a one-conjunct mutation in Vhs tape tokenize() can spin FOREVER on
        input already present in this suite: without a limit that run never ends
        and never reports, so the whole suite reads as "still going" rather than
        "broken" (measured: such a run had to be killed externally at 180s).
        60s was picked off measurement, not taste. A JUnit-logged run of
        all 6465 tests on this host (PHP 8.3.6) peaks at 9.321s
        (Tools\WebSearchToolTest::testHandlesRedirectResponse), with 4 tests over
        5s and none over 10s. So the bound sits ~6x above the slowest real test:
        loose enough that host load or a slow network redirect cannot trip it,
        tight enough that an infinite loop reports in a minute. Re-measure with
        phpunit's JUnit log flag if that peak ever moves.

        WHAT THE TIME LIMIT DOES AND DOES NOT BOUND, measured on this host rather
        than assumed. Three runaway shapes were probed under this config: a pure
        spin loop aborted at 60.09s; a loop raising a PHP warning every iteration
        aborted at 61.4s having banked 2,640,658 warnings; a loop appending 4KB
        strings forever aborted at 65.6s. So the alarm reports for all three. But
        a reviewer measured a real tokenize() mutant that ran past 600s with RSS
        over 4GB and produced ONE progress character in ten minutes — under enough
        memory pressure the abort's own teardown does not complete promptly, and
        the practical failure mode becomes "the CI job hangs until the CI timeout".
        The limit is therefore a bound on TIME SPENT COMPUTING, not a bound on a
        process that has started thrashing.

        Which is why memory_limit is set below. PHP's CLI default here is -1, so
        an allocating runaway grows until the machine swaps. Measured on the same
        4KB-append loop: unlimited took 65.6s (38s of it in sys, i.e. paging);
        at 1G it dies in 0.626s with "Allowed memory size of 1073741824 bytes
        exhausted". Same defect, same red, two orders of magnitude less damage.
        1G is ~4x the whole suite's measured peak of 253.25MB, so it has room to
        spare for legitimate work; raise it if that peak ever approaches it.

        failOnRisky is what makes the limit bite. PHPUnit records a timed-out test
        as RISKY, and risky alone still exits 0 — measured: the abort printed
        "This test was aborted after 60 seconds" under a green "OK, but there were
        issues!" banner. So without failOnRisky the hang-catcher is a notice CI
        scrolls past. Safe to turn on here because the suite reports 0 risky
        tests today (6465 tests, 1 skipped, 0 risky).

        No test declares #[Small]/#[Medium]/#[Large], so defaultTimeLimit is the
        limit every test actually gets. Add a size attribute anywhere and that
        test silently switches to PHPUnit's own 1s/10s/60s instead.

        Known gap, deliberately accepted: php-invoker enforces this with
        pcntl_alarm(), and tests/Agents/AgentWorkerPoolTest.php arms and cancels
        pcntl_alarm() itself. Its pcntl_alarm(0) calls clear the enforcing alarm,
        so the limit does not apply inside those tests. They are bounded by their
        own alarms instead, which is why they are also the tests least in need of
        this one.
    -->
    <php>
        <ini name="memory_limit" value="1G"/>
    </php>
    <testsuites>
        <testsuite name="SugarCrush">
            <directory>tests</directory>
        </testsuite>
    </testsuites>
    <source>
        <include>
            <directory>src</directory>
        </include>
    </source>
</phpunit>
